### The parent wants to end comfort bottles even though the child can already drink from cups.

3 of 18 posts.

These parents of two-year-olds, including a 2.25-year-old, describe cup competence alongside a specific attachment to bottles for milk, sleep or soothing. They have watered milk down, offered familiar cups, poured the same milk between containers or reduced bottles gradually. Questions range from stopping abruptly to using a bottle fairy and changing bedtime at the same time.

- "I’ve started watering down his milk in the morning and sometimes I offer it to him in a straw cup but he gets so pissed. Should I just cold turkey cut him off bottles? He drinks from open cup and straw cup just fine through the day." [ar061-0033]
- "Tonight, we even did an experiment where he poured the milk directly from his bottle into his favorite sippy cup. He took one sip and made a completely disgusted face. He genuinely hated it, even though the milk tasted fine. It seems like a pure sensory/comfort association with the bottle itself." [ar006-0129]
- "We were down to just the nap time one and a bottle full of water at night, but randomly around 1.5 she got super re-attached and just will not be redirected or soothed if she doesn’t have one." [ar046-0123]
- "Our plan is to do a variation of the Swedish bottle fairy idea to get her to give up bottles semi-voluntarily. This plan focuses on the idea of “graduating” to be a big girl and passing on the bottles to babies" [ar046-0124]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1tg2o8n 1upjcuq 1x8on3z

### The parent wants to replace bottles with cups but the child refuses the alternatives.

3 of 18 posts.

These posts concern a two-year-old, a child aged 2 years 10 months and an almost-three-year-old. Parents describe rejected cups and bottles kept for milk, water, nighttime or soothing; one has bought more than fifty cup types and waited through a tantrum that ended in vomiting. One parent compares medical advice to wean with reassurance from people around them that older bottle use is ordinary.

- "Yes I've rode out the tantrum he goes until he throws up it's terrible. I've given him just water in his bottles (tip from my mom) and he didn't care, he just wants his bottle." [ar051-0062]
- "I tried to get him used to drink out of a cute cup but he denies. […] He‘s not a very good eater (especially in the evening), he prefers his milk bottle." [ar074-0099]
- "My almost three year old daughter refuses to give up her Dr. Browns bottle. She only drinks water out of it, but refuses to use a sippy cup. She also likes to go to bed with it at night and drinks water out of it while sleeping." [ar027-0144]
- "was there a cup that clicked for your bottle obsessed toddlers? how do I get him off of the bottle without the fits, I know I have to do this ASAP but I can't stand him working himself up so badly." [ar051-0063]

**But:** Cup refusal is not absolute in every post: "He will accept drinking water from either an open cup or from my water bottle, but only a sip or two and then he'll ask for his baba and throw a fit until he gets it." [ar051-0062]

Posts: 1v95ssd 1vhcytr 1wv8a5o

### The parent wants independent eating after hospital stays made feeding the child a habit.

2 of 18 posts.

Both parents describe two-year-olds who can feed themselves but are still frequently fed by a parent after hospital care. One links the change to two hospitalizations, weight worries and survival while working, with current molar pain making meals worse; the other began hand-feeding around another brain surgery at about 20 months. They want to change the habit but fear inadequate intake or the child becoming hard to regulate emotionally.

- "Then he was hospitalised twice over a span of 5 months and regressed to being spoon-fed. We were super stressed about his overall weight and development, so we did whatever we could to feed him whilst working full-time jobs. What was survival mode turned into a regular thing." [ar079-0033]
- "He’s totally capable of feeding himself, but I don’t know how to break the cycle. I know I could just put the food down and let him eat if he’s hungry. But then he’ll turn into a moody mess. I think he has a harder time regulating his emotions due to his history with his brain surgeries. I’m just at a loss" [ar031-0045]
- "Gaaah, I am so scared to transition into self-feeding and be okay with him not eating properly because I'm so used to making sure every bite gets into him." [ar079-0038]
- "I don't regret it (if you're going to judge, please skip this post), but now would like to work towards more independent feeding." [ar079-0034]

**But:** The children do show independent eating in some circumstances: "He will feed himself if it’s a preferred food like pizza, pickles, anything like that." [ar031-0043] The other parent also says: "Sometimes he'll feed himself, but he was eating quite well until the molars started coming." [ar079-0031]

Posts: 1tt2c70 1uo58lq

### The parent wonders whether feeding a formerly independent child provides connection.

1 of 18 posts.

In this post about a two-year-old, the parent has begun feeding a daughter who used to be independent. The parent names mom guilt, wonders whether the request is for connection and asks whether others see the same thing; the post does not establish the child’s motive.

- "I’ve refused to feed her most times because she used to be super independent" [ar062-0105]
- "recently I’ve been feeling mom guilt" [ar062-0105]
- "maybe she’s just wanting connection so I been feeding her. Anyone else?" [ar062-0105]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1vwpie1

### The parent wants direct breastfeeding to work because pumping is wearing them down.

1 of 18 posts.

The parent of an 11-week-old born at 36 weeks 5 days describes early latch attempts escalating the baby’s hunger and frustration. After roughly two months of exclusive pumping without much further latch practice, the parent asks whether breastfeeding can still be established around three months. Feeding, pumping and housework are taking a toll on the parent’s mental health.

- "Early on I tried to get him to latch before feeding him expressed milk but it wasn't really working out - he'd go from 0 to 60 so quick with his hunger cues, and his frustration from not latching made it so much worse." [ar049-0051]
- "So I've basically been EP for two months without really trying to get him to latch." [ar049-0051]
- "The thing is, it's really starting to take a toll on my mental health - if I'm not feeding him from bottle, I'm pumping." [ar049-0052]
- "So have any of you had any luck getting baby to latch at around 3 months if they've been bottle fed EBM from the jump? Or am I out of luck?" [ar049-0052]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1ur6k2q

### The parent shares a later move from bottles to breastfeeding to challenge early pressure.

1 of 18 posts.

Writing at 12 weeks postpartum, this parent describes mostly bottle-feeding pumped milk for the first five or six weeks, followed by mostly breastfeeding by six weeks. Nipple shields and bottle-feeding in a breastfeeding position accompanied the transition, while the father continues a daily bottle. The parent objects to early pressure and judgment and asks why parents cannot receive help with a later transition.

- "I absolutely hated the pressure to breastfeed at the beginning, and all of the judgement when you tell people you’re not doing it." [ar059-0008]
- "So I got some nipple shields to help him latch, had him in a BF position with the bottle by my boob when he was being fed with pumped milk, and then by 6 weeks he was mostly breastfeeding (his dad still gives him a bottle every day because it’s nice for them to have that time together)." [ar059-0007]
- "Why not ease that pressure, let both parents help with the feeding at the beginning when hormones are high and sleep hours are low, and then assist with the transition from bottle to boob later on?" [ar059-0008]

**But:** The parent explicitly limits the example’s reach: "I understand that doing things this way around may not work for everyone" [ar059-0008]

Posts: 1sfn2ef

### The parent needs a breastfed baby to accept bottles and asks what else to try.

2 of 18 posts.

These parents of nine- and ten-week-olds describe bottle refusal with different starting points: one baby previously accepted a daily expressed-milk bottle, while the other will not accept bottles despite many approaches. Attempts include teat-flow changes, four bottle brands, temperatures, positions and different caregivers. One parent wants to stop breastfeeding because of its personal toll and move from expressed milk to hypoallergenic formula for reported CMPA; the other wants bottles available for the husband’s feeds and time away.

- "Breastfeeding is slowly destroying me and I can't keep doing it but I feel like I have no choice with the bottle refusal." [ar030-0111]
- "This week, baby suddenly started refusing the bottle. We've never had this issue before, he'd always taken it without fuss." [ar021-0154]
- "We've tried various brands (Philips Avent, MAM, lanisoh and nuk) as well as different teat flows. Experimented with different temperatures. I've attempted to feed, as has my husband and my mother. We've tried sitting up position, lie-down feeding, I've tried nursing and then doing the ol'switcheroo with the bottle and she will just not take it." [ar030-0109]
- "Ive tried changing from extra slow teats to just slow flow and its made no difference." [ar021-0154]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1w6iqei 1w8xgc8

### The parent wants a bottle flow that lets the baby feed without falling asleep or choking.

1 of 18 posts.

After eight weeks of breastfeeding and a return to work, this parent reports that the daughter now falls asleep after half an ounce with a Lansinoh extra-slow nipple. Moving to the slow flow seems to choke and upset her. The parent asks for a compatible intermediate option and says they may need to leave work after intake of only 0.75 ounces in five hours.

- "She did okay last week with the Lansinoh XS flow nipple but now she is falling asleep while feeding after only 0.5 oz." [ar074-0112]
- "I upped to the S flow nipple and it seems to be choking her up and she gets upset." [ar074-0112]
- "Are there any other brands of nipples that fit lansinoh bottles that are kind of an in between flow that your babies have liked?" [ar074-0112]
- "I’m about to have to leave work because she’s only eaten 0.75oz in 5 hours and seems to not want any more." [ar074-0112]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1vy0zll

### The parent fears choking when a capable eater stuffs too much food into the mouth.

1 of 18 posts.

The parent of a child who turned two a month earlier describes fast eating, large bites and mouth-stuffing during excitement or play. Modelling slow eating and removing food have not resolved it, and the parent says the child mimics Cookie Monster. The parent wants to teach safer eating and repeatedly names fear of choking.

- "Thankfully, he has not choked yet, but I’m so terrified he will." [ar032-0027]
- "I’ve tried consequences recently, by taking his food away when he doesn’t stop, but has yet to be effective." [ar032-0027]
- "Like today, he had avocado toast pieces and he decided he wanted to make a sandwich out of then and stacked them together and then shoved all the pieces (so basically a whole piece of toast ) in his mouth." [ar032-0029]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1sp3kjz

### The parent questions choking warnings after seeing other parents offer whole meat sticks.

1 of 18 posts.

This parent of a two-year-old has not offered meat sticks and normally quarters foods such as hot dogs. Seeing other parents offer whole meat sticks to children aged two or younger leads them to compare that practice with warnings to wait until four. The parent asks whether the warnings are overly cautious or whether the whole food is actually safe, describing the question as curiosity.

- "Everywhere I look, it explicitly says that it's a HUGE choking risk and you should never give a whole Chomps to a 2yo, and shouldn't until they're 4yo." [ar068-0091]
- "I've been seeing a few different posts around reddit where a parent will give their 2yo or under a whole meat stick, like Chomps. I haven't given my 2yo a meat stick yet, but have always quartered up things like hot dogs when it's for lunch/dinner." [ar068-0090]
- "My question is.. are all these sources erring on the major side of caution, or is it actually safe to give them whole meat sticks?" [ar068-0092]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1wdzhv1

## Pattern 7, share 09: near posts

### The parent wants more food accepted but worries about withholding alternatives.

1 of 4 posts.

This parent describes a two-year-old with a dependable breakfast and snack, many refused foods and occasional small amounts of others. They have offered varied tastes and textures, had ties released and feeding and speech assessed, and often make an alternative when family food is thrown. The husband favors fewer choices and going hungry if needed, while the parent worries about a child at the fifth weight percentile, disrupted sleep and wasted homemade food; a sister advises waiting for adjustment to preschool.

- "We have been giving him a variety of food and textures, introducing him to spices and foods of different temperatures from the start, but we don't seem to be making any progress.  Every meal is stressing me out." [ar021-0136]
- "I would love to just give him what we are having, but when I have tried, he just throws it and then won't eat it, and then I cave and make him something else." [ar021-0143]
- "He thinks he will eat when he gets hungry enough, and if he doesn't eat the chili we are having, for instance, then I should put him to bed hungry and he will figure it out the next day." [ar021-0144]
- "Our little guy is healthy and happy, but he is tiny (5th percentile for weight), so I get worried about putting him to bed with no food - but maybe the learning curve would be quick, and I should get over it?" [ar021-0145]

**But:** A previous feeding assessment did not identify a mouth-movement problem: "they watched him eat and said his mouth was moving well and there weren't any issues" [ar021-0138] The child also has some reliably accepted food: "He loves breakfast and eats it every day" [ar021-0139]

Posts: 1vvu20t

### The parent wants possible allergy investigated after feeding improved and then became uncomfortable.

1 of 4 posts.

The parent of a six-week-old describes hospital feeding support after a 14% weight loss, followed by improved breastfeeding after a posterior tongue-tie release at ten days. More recent blood and mucus in stools, vomiting, rash and distressed shorter feeds lead the parent to ask about CMPA investigation and dairy elimination. A midwife raised possible intolerance, while the GP was unconcerned about CMPA because the baby was settling and gaining weight.

- "His feeding has also changed, he can seem very hungry but then within 30 seconds–1 minute of latching he becomes frantic, arches/straightens his body, cries and comes off, so feeds are often much shorter and more frequent and I’m struggling to get him satisfied as he’s just too uncomfortable." [ar063-0097]
- "I did ask about CMPA, but the GP said he wouldn’t be concerned because he would expect a baby with it to be completely inconsolable, covered in rashes and losing weight — which isn’t the case with my baby." [ar063-0098]
- "Does this sound like symptoms worth pushing to investigate, even though he is generally able to be settled eventually and gaining weight? And is a dairy elimination trial something I should be asking about?" [ar063-0099]

**But:** The account also reports substantial improvement after release and good weight gain: "After that, breastfeeding improved massively. He started latching well, feeding efficiently, coming off satisfied and gaining weight really well." [ar063-0095]

Posts: 1w2s888

### The parent wants direct breastfeeding to work because pumping is wearing them down.

1 of 4 posts.

At ten weeks postpartum, this parent describes a shallow, painful latch, an unhelpful hospital lactation visit while recovering from a birth injury, and weeks of exclusive pumping with occasional nursing. They name exhaustion, anger about pumping and washing dishes, concern about dwindling supply and lack of time for repeated consultant visits. They still strongly want to give breastmilk until nine months or a year and anticipate hating themselves for stopping.

- "I hate pumping. I want to nurse my baby so bad but her latch is so bad and so shallow and it hurts so much." [ar081-0112]
- "I’m so fucjing tired and so upset. I’m so sick of washing the fcking dishes all the time and all the other bullshit that comes along with being an exclusive pumper." [ar081-0113]
- "And I don’t have time to see a freaking lactation consultant. I’d probably have to see them multiple fcking times in order to get her to nurse properly and I don’t want to do that!!!!" [ar081-0113]
- "I hate this so much but I think I would hate myself for quitting even more." [ar081-0115]

**But:** Direct nursing is possible occasionally, despite the painful latch: "I nurse her every once in a while in a pinch or if I really don’t want to pump." [ar081-0113]

Posts: 1w8mkyo

### The parent asks whether painful newborn breastfeeding means the latch needs help or feeding should change.

1 of 4 posts.

A first-time mother of a two-day-old, nearly three-day-old newborn describes painful breastfeeding, raw nipples, some cracking and cluster feeding that leaves little sleep. She has used nipple cream and silver cups and ordered shields and heat/cooling pads for same-day delivery. She asks whether this is normal, whether she should endure it, or whether to supplement or stop.

- "I’m worried I’m doing it all wrong (as we do!) and knew breastfeeding wouldn’t be pleasant." [ar081-0122]
- "Left nipple has some cracking, right looks fine. Both are pretty okay looking but feel so very raw afterwards and deeply painful when he feeds." [ar081-0123]
- "I’ve ordered all the things for same day delivery (shields, heat/cooling pads, etc.) but I’m worried it’s my latch or something. Has anyone had anything similar? Is this just normal? Do I need to just get through it? Is it a sign I should stop or add formula to the mix? Any reassurance or advice would be amazing." [ar081-0124]

**But:** The mother also reports a successful latch earlier: "after the first home visit where he latched perfectly" [ar081-0122]

Posts: 1wb7n2r

